IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ea-cpc-interface`
IMPORT `ea-types`

-- Evidence Act 1893, Part 1, sections 34 to 57: records, how much of a statement is proved,
-- judgments and convictions, opinions of third persons, and character.
-- Source: source/EA1893.txt lines 1586 to 2019.  Sections 35 and 36 are repealed (Act 4 of 2012);
-- section 36A is a rule-making power (rules for filing and receiving evidence by information
-- technology) and is not encoded.
--
-- The questions this module answers are the remaining routes by which a fact, a document or an
-- opinion becomes relevant, and so feed the field `the fact is declared relevant by another
-- provision of the Act` in ea-relevancy.l4: records (34 to 41), judgments and convictions (42 to
-- 46), opinions (47 to 53), character (54 to 57).

§ `34 to 41. Statements in records, maps, recitals and law books`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 34"
GIVEN r IS A `Record Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the entry in the books of accounts is relevant under section 34` IF
        r's `the entry is in books of accounts regularly kept in the course of business`
    AND r's `the entry refers to a matter into which the court has to inquire`

-- 34: such statements "are not alone sufficient evidence to charge any person with liability".
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 34"
GIVEN r IS A `Record Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the entry is sufficient to charge a person with liability` IF
        `the entry in the books of accounts is relevant under section 34` r
    AND r's `there is other evidence to charge the person with liability`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 37"
GIVEN r IS A `Record Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the entry in the public record is relevant under section 37` IF
        r's `the entry is in a public or other official book, register or record`
    AND r's `the entry states a fact in issue or a relevant fact`
    AND r's `the entry was made by a public officer in the discharge of official duty, or by another person in performance of a duty specially enjoined by the law of the country in which the book, register or record is kept`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 38"
GIVEN r IS A `Record Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement in the map, chart or plan is relevant under section 38` IF
        r's `the statement is made in a published map or chart generally offered for public sale`
    OR  r's `the statement is made in a map or plan made under the authority of the Government as to matters usually represented or stated in such maps or plans`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 39(a) to (d)"
GIVEN r IS A `Record Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the recital is relevant under section 39` IF
        r's `the court has to form an opinion as to the existence of a fact of a public nature`
    AND (   r's `the statement is made in a recital in an Act or Ordinance`
         OR r's `the statement is made in a recital in legislation enacted by the Parliament of Malaysia or the legislature of a part of the Commonwealth`
         OR r's `the statement is made in a recital in legislation enacted by the legislature of a State of Malaysia`
         OR r's `the statement is made in a recital in a document purporting to be the Government Gazette, the London Gazette, or a Gazette of Malaysia or of a part of the Commonwealth`)

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 40"
GIVEN r IS A `Record Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement of foreign law is relevant under section 40` IF
        r's `the court has to form an opinion as to a law of a country`
    AND (   r's `the statement is contained in a book purporting to be printed or published under the authority of the government of that country and to contain that law`
         OR r's `the report is of a ruling of the courts of that country and is contained in a book purporting to be a report of the rulings`)

-- 41: evidence is to be given of so much of the longer statement, conversation, document, book or
-- series, "and no more", as the court considers necessary in the particular case to the full
-- understanding of the nature and effect of the statement and the circumstances in which it was made.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 41"
GIVEN r IS A `Record Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the whole of the longer statement or document must be proved under section 41` IF
        r's `the statement forms part of a longer statement, a conversation, an isolated document, or a document forming part of a book or of a connected series of letters or papers`
    AND r's `the court considers the whole of it necessary in that particular case to the full understanding of the nature and effect of the statement and of the circumstances in which it was made`

§ `42 to 46. Judgments, orders and decrees`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 42"
GIVEN j IS A `Judgment Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the judgment is relevant under section 42` IF
        j's `the judgment, order or decree by law prevents a court from taking cognizance of a suit or holding a trial`
    AND j's `the question is whether the court ought to take cognizance of the suit or hold the trial`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 43(1)"
GIVEN j IS A `Judgment Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the judgment is relevant under section 43(1)` IF
        j's `the judgment, order or decree is a final one of a competent court in the exercise of probate, matrimonial, admiralty or bankruptcy jurisdiction`
    AND j's `it confers on or takes away from a person a legal character, or declares a person entitled to a legal character or to a specific thing, not as against a specified person but absolutely`
    AND j's `the existence of that legal character, or the title of that person to that thing, is relevant`

-- 43(2): such a judgment is conclusive proof of the matters in (a) to (d): when the legal character
-- accrued, when it was to cease, and when the thing became the person's property.  Conclusive proof
-- is the first kind of presumption in section 4(3): see ea-presumptions-burden.l4.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 43(2)(a) to (d)"
GIVEN j IS A `Judgment Facts`
GIVETH A `Kind of Presumption`
`the effect of a section 43 judgment` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the judgment is relevant under section 43(1)` j THEN `conclusive proof`
           OTHERWISE REFUSE "section 43(2): the judgment is not within section 43(1), so section 43(2) says nothing about its effect"

-- 44: judgments other than those in 43 are relevant if they relate to matters of a public nature
-- relevant to the inquiry, but are not conclusive proof of what they state.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 44"
GIVEN j IS A `Judgment Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the judgment is relevant under section 44` IF
        NOT `the judgment is relevant under section 43(1)` j
    AND j's `the judgment, order or decree relates to matters of a public nature relevant to the inquiry`

-- 45: judgments other than those in 42, 43 and 44 are irrelevant unless their existence is a fact
-- in issue or they are relevant under some other provision.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 45"
GIVEN j IS A `Judgment Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the other judgment is relevant under section 45` IF
        NOT `the judgment is relevant under section 42` j
    AND NOT `the judgment is relevant under section 43(1)` j
    AND NOT `the judgment is relevant under section 44` j
    AND (   j's `the existence of the judgment, order or decree is a fact in issue`
         OR j's `the judgment, order or decree is relevant under another provision of the Act`)

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, ss 42 to 45"
GIVEN j IS A `Judgment Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the judgment, order or decree is relevant` IF
        `the judgment is relevant under section 42` j
    OR  `the judgment is relevant under section 43(1)` j
    OR  `the judgment is relevant under section 44` j
    OR  `the other judgment is relevant under section 45` j

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 46"
GIVEN j IS A `Judgment Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `fraud, collusion or incompetence of the court may be shown under section 46` IF
        j's `the judgment, order or decree is relevant under section 42, 43 or 44 and has been proved by the adverse party`
    AND j's `it was delivered by a court not competent to deliver it, or was obtained by fraud or collusion`

§ `45A. Relevance of convictions and acquittals`

-- 45A(1): without affecting sections 42 to 45, the fact that a person has been convicted or
-- acquitted of an offence by or before a court in Singapore is admissible to prove, where relevant to
-- an issue in the proceedings, that the person committed (or did not commit) the offence, whether or
-- not the person is a party, and whether the conviction was on a plea of guilty or otherwise.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 45A(1)"
GIVEN c IS A `Conviction Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the conviction or acquittal is admissible under section 45A(1)` IF
        c's `the person has been convicted or acquitted of an offence by or before a court in Singapore`
    AND c's `the conviction or acquittal is relevant to an issue in the proceedings`

-- 45A(2): a conviction referred to in subsection (1) is relevant and admissible unless it is subject
-- to review or appeal not yet determined, it has been quashed or set aside, or a pardon has been
-- given.  45A(7): in criminal proceedings the section is subject to any written law or rule of law
-- that a conviction is not admissible to prove a tendency or disposition on the part of the accused
-- to commit the kind of offence charged.  The text of (2) speaks of a "conviction" only, and the
-- three exceptions are therefore applied to a conviction, not to an acquittal: see NOTES.md, fork F5.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 45A(2) and (7)"
GIVEN c IS A `Conviction Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the conviction is relevant and admissible under section 45A(2)` IF
        `the conviction or acquittal is admissible under section 45A(1)` c
    AND NOT c's `the conviction is subject to review or appeal that has not yet been determined`
    AND NOT c's `the conviction has been quashed or set aside`
    AND NOT c's `a pardon has been given in respect of the conviction`
    AND NOT (   c's `the kind of proceedings` EQUALS `criminal proceedings`
            AND c's `a written law or other rule of law provides that a conviction is not admissible to prove a tendency or disposition on the part of the accused to commit the kind of offence charged`)

-- 45A(3): a person proved to have been convicted is, unless the contrary is proved, taken to have
-- committed the acts and to have possessed the state of mind (if any) that at law constitute the
-- offence.  "Unless the contrary is proved" makes it a rebuttable presumption.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 45A(3)"
GIVEN c IS A `Conviction Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is taken to have committed the acts and possessed the state of mind of the offence` IF
    `the conviction is relevant and admissible under section 45A(2)` c

-- 45A(4): a conviction or acquittal may be proved by a certificate signed by the registrar giving the
-- substance and effect of the charge and of the conviction or acquittal; 45A(6): in addition to any
-- other authorised manner of proof.  45A(5): where relevant, a document containing the details of the
-- information, complaint, charge, agreed statement of facts or record of proceedings is admissible.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 45A(4)"
GIVEN c IS A `Conviction Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the conviction or acquittal may be proved by certificate` IF
        `the conviction or acquittal is admissible under section 45A(1)` c
    AND c's `a certificate of conviction or acquittal is signed by the Registrar of the Supreme Court, the registrar of the State Courts or the registrar of the Family Justice Courts and gives the substance and effect of the charge and of the conviction or acquittal`

§ `47 to 53. Opinions of third persons`

-- 47(1): when the court is likely to derive assistance from an opinion on a point of scientific,
-- technical or other specialised knowledge, the opinions of experts are relevant facts.  47(2): an
-- expert is a person with such knowledge based on training, study or experience.  47(3): the opinion
-- is not irrelevant merely because it relates to a matter of common knowledge (inert).  47(4): not
-- relevant if the court is of the view that it would not be in the interests of justice.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 47(1), (2) and (4)"
GIVEN o IS AN `Opinion Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the opinion of an expert is relevant under section 47` IF
        o's `the court is likely to derive assistance from an opinion upon a point of scientific, technical or other specialised knowledge`
    AND o's `the person has scientific, technical or other specialised knowledge based on training, study or experience`
    AND NOT o's `the court is of the view that it would not be in the interests of justice to treat the opinion as relevant`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 48"
GIVEN o IS AN `Opinion Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the fact is relevant under section 48` IF
    o's `the fact supports or is inconsistent with a relevant opinion of an expert`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 49 and Explanation"
GIVEN o IS AN `Opinion Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the opinion on handwriting is relevant under section 49` IF
        o's `the court has to form an opinion as to the person by whom a document was written or signed`
    AND o's `the opinion is that of a person acquainted with the handwriting of the person by whom the document is supposed to be written or signed`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 50"
GIVEN o IS AN `Opinion Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the opinion on a general custom or right is relevant under section 50` IF
        o's `the court has to form an opinion as to the existence of a general custom or right`
    AND o's `the opinion is that of a person who would be likely to know of its existence if it existed`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 51(a), (b), (c)"
GIVEN o IS AN `Opinion Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the opinion on usages, tenets or meanings is relevant under section 51` IF
        o's `the court has to form an opinion as to the usages and tenets of a body of men or family, the constitution and government of a religious or charitable foundation, or the meaning of words or terms used in particular districts or by particular classes of people`
    AND o's `the opinion is that of a person having special means of knowledge on the subject`

-- 52(2): the opinion expressed by conduct on a relationship is not sufficient to prove a marriage in
-- prosecutions under section 6A of the Women's Charter 1961.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 52(1) and (2)"
GIVEN o IS AN `Opinion Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the opinion expressed by conduct on a relationship is relevant under section 52` IF
        o's `the court has to form an opinion as to the relationship of one person to another`
    AND o's `the opinion is expressed by conduct of a person who as a member of the family or otherwise has special means of knowledge on the subject`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 52(2)"
GIVEN o IS AN `Opinion Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the opinion is sufficient to prove a marriage` IF
        `the opinion expressed by conduct on a relationship is relevant under section 52` o
    AND NOT o's `the prosecution is under section 6A of the Women's Charter 1961 and the question is whether there was a marriage`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 53"
GIVEN o IS AN `Opinion Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the grounds of the opinion are relevant under section 53` IF
    o's `the opinion of a living person is relevant`

§ `54 to 57. Character`

-- 54: in civil cases the fact that a person's character is such as to render probable or improbable
-- conduct imputed to the person is irrelevant, except insofar as the character appears from facts
-- otherwise relevant.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 54"
GIVEN c IS A `Character Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the character evidence is irrelevant under section 54` IF
        c's `the kind of proceedings` EQUALS `civil proceedings`
    AND c's `the fact is that the character of a person is such as to render probable or improbable conduct imputed to the person`
    AND NOT c's `the character appears from facts otherwise relevant`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 55"
GIVEN c IS A `Character Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the accused's good character is relevant under section 55` IF
        c's `the kind of proceedings` EQUALS `criminal proceedings`
    AND c's `the fact is that the accused is of a good character`

-- 56(1) and (2): in criminal proceedings, if the accused asks questions of a witness, gives evidence
-- or calls a witness to establish good disposition or reputation, the prosecution, and a person
-- jointly charged, may call evidence that the accused is of bad disposition or reputation and may
-- cross-examine to establish it.  The section is not limited to the prosecution: a person jointly
-- charged is given the same entitlement, and so is covered by the same field.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 56(1) and (2)"
GIVEN c IS A `Character Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `evidence of bad disposition or reputation may be called under section 56(2)` IF
        c's `the kind of proceedings` EQUALS `criminal proceedings`
    AND (   c's `the accused or the accused's advocate asked a witness questions with a view to establishing that the accused is of good disposition or reputation`
         OR c's `the accused gave evidence tending to establish that the accused is of good disposition or reputation`
         OR c's `the accused called a witness to give evidence tending to establish that the accused is of good disposition or reputation`)
    AND c's `the party seeking to call the evidence is the prosecution or a person jointly charged with the accused`

-- 56(3): where a party is entitled to call such evidence, the party may call evidence of the accused's
-- previous convictions, if any, whether or not the party calls any other evidence for that purpose.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 56(3)"
GIVEN c IS A `Character Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `evidence of previous convictions may be called under section 56(3)` IF
        `evidence of bad disposition or reputation may be called under section 56(2)` c
    AND c's `the accused has previous convictions`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 57"
GIVEN c IS A `Character Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the character evidence is relevant under section 57` IF
        c's `the kind of proceedings` EQUALS `civil proceedings`
    AND c's `the fact is that the character of a person is such as to affect the amount of damages the person ought to receive`

-- The Explanation to section 57 applies to sections 54 to 57: "character" includes reputation and
-- disposition, but except as provided in section 56, evidence may be given only of general reputation
-- and general disposition, and not of particular acts by which they are shown.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 57 Explanation"
GIVEN c IS A `Character Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the form of the character evidence is permitted by the Explanation to section 57` IF
        c's `the evidence is of general reputation and general disposition and not of particular acts by which reputation or disposition are shown`
    OR  `evidence of bad disposition or reputation may be called under section 56(2)` c
